ella kissy debra was a keen swimmer  dancer and cyclist  by the age of nine she had the reading  age of a teenager but she never made it  to her 10th birthday  over a three-year period she suffered  numerous seizures going into hospital 27  times with breathing problems an inquest  in 2014 found  ella died of acute respiratory failure  after a severe asthma attack  but that was quashed when new evidence  emerged about local air pollution  her mother wants air pollution recorded  as the cause of death  something that's never happened before  in the uk for the first six years of her  life  there is no warning ever of how  everything was going to end hopefully  from this case we will learn a lot more  about air pollution and the impact  on health and governments everywhere  will take action  so no children will have to go through  what my late daughter went through  the new inquest is also considering what  steps were taken to reduce air pollution  and to inform the public  experts say pollution levels at a  monitoring station here in catford near  ella's home  consistently exceeded legal limits in  the years up to her death  most of ella's half-hour walk to school  was along this highly congested road  two years after she died an expert  report concluded  there was a direct link between her  condition  and levels of nitrogen dioxide and  airborne particles in this area  the inquest could set a precedent  helping people  hold local and central government to  account for the air they breathe  rosamund kisidebra was never told about  the risks of air pollution to her  daughter  but clean air campaigners say there's  now plenty of evidence it's cutting  short thousands of lives across the uk  it causes and makes worse a whole host  of diseases  from asthma to heart attacks to  cardiovascular disease to dementia  and we know that it impacts the most  vulnerable the hardest as well so that's  children  older people and also those on lower  incomes  the local authority where rosamund lives  has admitted it should have treated  pollution levels as a public health  emergency  she's hoping the inquest into ella's  death can make everybody think about its  impact  and take action to save lives nadine  barber al jazeera  london
 

